R.4  THE ADDRESS AS A PATH, AND WHAT THE BOUNDARIES DICTATE ALONG IT
     An address of depth n is an oriented path-word of length n
     (Definition 26.1), and it resides within the descent
     independently of any act of selection, retrieval or
     observation (Theorem 15.1).  There is no act of obtaining it to
     be priced: the cost of a configuration is the length of its
     path.  What follows is therefore not a search.  It is each
     standing address written along its own path, with the boundary
     that dictates the orientation named at every position it bears
     on.

     FFFTTT   a path of length 6
       x1=F   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x2=F   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x3=F   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x4=T   dictated by u6   (x1 v x3 v x4)
       x5=T   dictated by u10   (x2 v !x4 v x5)
       x6=T   dictated by u5   (x1 v x2 v x6)

     FTFTTF   a path of length 6
       x1=F   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x2=T   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x3=F   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x4=T   dictated by u6   (x1 v x3 v x4)
       x5=T   dictated by u4   (x1 v !x2 v x5)
       x6=F   dictated by u8   (!x2 v !x4 v !x6)

     TTTFFT   a path of length 6
       x1=T   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x2=T   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x3=T   dictated by u0   (!x1 v !x2 v x3)
       x4=F   no single boundary bears on it at this prefix
       x5=F   dictated by u7   (!x2 v !x3 v !x5)
       x6=T   dictated by u3   (!x1 v x4 v x6)

       positions along the 3 paths            18
       dictated by a named boundary               9
       not borne on by any single boundary        9

    PASS  every standing address is an oriented path-word of length n   [3 addresses, depth 6]
    PASS  wherever a boundary bears on a position it dictates the orientation the address carries there   [9 positions dictated, none in disagreement]
    PASS  the positions no single boundary bears on are the measure of the assertion, not of a search   [9 of 18 dictated by the m separate boundaries; composing them at depth is what would dictate the rest]

     The last figure is the one to read.  Where a position is
     dictated, the boundary already holds the orientation and no
     act determines it.  Where none of the m separate boundaries
     bears on a position, the assertion has not yet fixed it --
     the m clause boundaries have not been composed into one
     boundary at depth.  That is a property of the assertion made,
     and a better assertion makes a better boundary.

