R.6  THE ANSWER

     The boundaries admit 3 addresses of the 64 in the envelope:

       FFFTTT   x1=F   x2=F   x3=F   x4=T   x5=T   x6=T
       FTFTTF   x1=F   x2=T   x3=F   x4=T   x5=T   x6=F
       TTTFFT   x1=T   x2=T   x3=T   x4=F   x5=F   x6=T

     The planted assignment is FFFTTT, and it is among them.

     Each is checked clause by clause, every clause satisfied by a named literal:

       FFFTTT   !x1  !x1  !x1  !x1  !x2   x6   x4  !x2  !x2  !x3   x5
       FTFTTF   !x1  !x1  !x1  !x1   x5   x2   x4  !x3  !x6   x2   x2
       TTTFFT    x3   x3  !x4   x6   x1   x1   x1  !x5  !x4   x2   x2

    PASS  the addresses produced are exactly the committed solution set   [3 addresses, exact set equality]
    PASS  and exactly those a traversal of the whole envelope finds   [independent survey of all 64 addresses]
    PASS  every address produced satisfies every clause of the formula   3/3   [evaluated against the clauses, not the boundaries]
    PASS  no address outside the produced set satisfies the formula   [61 addresses outside, none satisfying]
    PASS  the planted assignment is produced

     The four questions of Theorem 9.1, put to the boundaries:
       how many addresses stand?           3
       does FFFTTT stand?                 yes, in 33 comparisons
